00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:41.620
So what is Cloud Firestore?

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.270
To put it simply,
Cloud Firestore

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.390
is a serverless, NoSQL,
document database

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:48.820
that is built for the cloud.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:50.710
But it's much more than that.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.400
Cloud Firestore is more of
a back-end as a service that

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.150
not only gives you the
database, but also provides

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.230
the infrastructure
that handles moving

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:02.530
stateful data between the
cloud and your application

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:06.580
and devices, which naturally
leads us into how it's also

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.400
an embedded database
that continues to power

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.420
your application and
devices to the cloud.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.580
So let's dive deeper
into Cloud Firestore.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.300
As mentioned, it's a
document-oriented database,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.730
which means that it's
designed to store and manage

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.640
semi-structured data,
so just JSON objects.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:28.960
It's completely schemaless.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.200
Meaning that you don't need
to design your schema upfront,

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.470
so that you can continue
to evolve your application

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:38.740
without having to worry about
coordinating structural changes

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.300
to your database.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.050
And you can update
your application

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.260
as and when you need it.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.200
Google fully manages the
infrastructure for you,

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.200
so that you can focus on
building your application

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.725
rather than thinking
about scheduling downtime,

00:05:52.725 --> 00:05:55.060
so you can install
your database upgrades

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.640
or even installing the latest
security vulnerability patches.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.340
It gives you a full
serverless experience,

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.560
so that you don't need to
think about machine sizes,

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:09.790
like CPU, RAMs, number of
nodes, replicas, disks,

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.560
or even networking.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.100
All of these are packaged with
pay-as-you-go pricing that can

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.110
scale down to zero
when you don't need it.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:22.600
Cloud Firestore is
super-simple to setup.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.300
You enable it from
your project, download

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.670
and install the
SDK in your apps,

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.980
and then you're ready to go.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.050
Like I said, it's more
than just a database.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.900
You can access it directly
from your application

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:37.180
without having to write
any middle tier that

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.100
sits in between.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.470
You can secure this
direct connection

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:44.500
with a combination of
declarative security rules

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.780
and integration with
Firebase authentication.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:53.140
What these rules allow is that
you can manage the request that

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.700
comes into Firestore.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.620
You can allow it
or deny it based

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.890
on what the request is,
who the sign-in user is,

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.080
and how it compares with
the information stored

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.060
in the database.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.390
And what makes Cloud
Firestore more than just

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:10.180
a database is the real-time
update capability.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:13.930
Cloud Firestore doesn't
give you just the database,

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:17.080
but also handles the pushing
of the updates, synchronization

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:21.670
of the changes from the database
with your app in real time.

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:23.170
So we're going to
see that in a demo

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:28.510
as well, which brings me to the
last embedded database piece.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:31.750
Firebase is the case for the
Firestore actively cached

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.870
data in your app, so
that you can write, read,

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:38.890
listen to, or query data, even
when the device is offline.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:43.000
The mobile and JavaScript SDKs
for iOS, Android, and browsers

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.340
give you the local
embedded database

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:49.540
that syncs with a cloud when
there's an active connection.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.410
It also has this nifty feature
called latency compensation,

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:57.850
which basically handles pushing
of the updates to the cloud,

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:00.640
and then unblocking the
UI and also giving you

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:04.540
more freedom in your app.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:06.480
Talking about
Firebase SDKs, there

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.700
are some crucial components
that are also included inside.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.250
The first is Firebase
authentication,

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:16.360
which supports signing in
user based on passwords, phone

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.610
numbers, and other popular
identity providers,

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:22.300
such as Google, Facebook,
Twitter, or GitHub.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.570
Security rules, as mentioned,
provide access control and data

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:29.650
validation in a simple
and yet expressive format.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.870
Cloud Functions
lets you configure

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:35.710
events, such as when you add
new document to your collection.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.330
And you can trigger a
function from there.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.470
So it lets you extend your
application even further

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:42.730
into the cloud.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.110
We have implemented some
Cloud Functions for the demo

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:48.400
later on for the real-time
object detection system,

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.650
and we're going
to see it shortly.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:54.550
Next, Firebase Cloud
Messaging lets you reliably

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:56.740
deliver messages at no cost.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.080
And lastly, Firebase
Remote Config,

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.600
it allows you to change
the behavior of your app

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.110
without requiring users
to download an app update.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:08.370
You can use this for scenarios,
such as A/B testing, doc

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:13.330
launches, canary release,
or feature toggle.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:15.920
And Cloud Firestore is not
just for Firebase project.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.530
You can use it for
server side as well.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:20.560
It's supports multiple
service or languages,

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.470
like C Sharp, Java, Go,
and so on and so forth.

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:26.320
It supports both
REST and gRPC APIs,

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.260
making it accessible
from any environment.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:32.020
It's designed for
reliability, with five

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:33.940
9's availability target.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.010
The multi-region
replication ensures

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:38.740
that your data is durable
and highly available,

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:42.160
even in the face of
the natural disasters.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.050
And it can scale
on demand, based

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:47.350
on the most demanding
workloads that you have,

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.500
so that you don't need to
think about connection pools.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.080
And it's highly suited
for scalable computing

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:55.510
infrastructure, such as
Kubernetes Engine or App

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:56.740
Engine.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:58.900
And you can both scale
up when you need it,

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:01.660
or scale down when
you don't need it.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:03.540
So I'll hand over
to Gopi, who will

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.040
