00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:14.385
So let's look at
the architecture

00:10:14.385 --> 00:10:16.280
and implementation.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:19.610
So if we look at this, we're
actually using Firebase.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:22.310
Firebase is like a
Swiss army knife.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:23.540
It has a file store.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:24.560
It gives you a storage.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:26.030
It gives you a cloud function.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:27.860
It gives you
hosting, and it also

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.040
give you authentication and
authorization capabilities.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.680
So what we're doing here
is, we have an IoT device,

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.770
which captures telemetry
data from the sensors mounted

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:40.220
on the [INAUDIBLE] and it
pushes the data in real time

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.270
to Cloud Firestore.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:46.240
On the other hand, the picture
captured from the camera module

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.110
is pushed to the
cloud storage, which

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.360
is basically Firebase Storage.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.340
So Firebase Storage
is essentially

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:54.350
a Google Cloud Storage
location, which

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:57.800
is accessible through
Firebase admin SDKs.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:02.030
So as the pictures keep arriving
in the Firebase Storage,

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:04.850
we actually written a simple
Cloud Function that gets

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:05.660
triggered.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:09.650
The Cloud Function basically
invokes region API calls.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:11.720
And all it does is
it detects objects

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:13.520
within the image,
in essence, labels

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.630
to their detected objects.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.080
So all these outputs are
basically written back

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.110
to the Cloud Firestore.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:25.160
The user interface basically is
hosted using Firebase Hosting.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.800
And all the authentication
is basically

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:29.885
done using Firebase
authentication.

00:11:29.885 --> 00:11:32.010
So there are a lot of
providers, which you can use.

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:33.620
So right now, in our
demo application,

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.140
we are using Google
Cloud auth sign-in

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:39.080
For authorization, we're
actually writing security rules

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.720
for authorizing
access to services,

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:44.330
like Firestore,
Storage, et cetera.

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.700
So that's the simplistic
version of the architecture

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:49.050
that we've developed.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:53.040
So you can basically extend
this simple architecture

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.730
to interact with your own fleet
of machine-learning services.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.120
So if you look at the
machine learning spectrum,

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:02.570
on the left-hand side,
you have a whole range

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:05.540
of ease-of-use services,
like Vision APIs.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:09.260
Or if you want to extend it more
customized to your use case,

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.390
you can basically
use Cloud RTML,

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:13.700
or you can further customize.

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:17.383
Bring your own data, build your
own models using Cloud Machine

00:12:17.383 --> 00:12:18.050
Learning Engine.

