00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:14.760
You can, as well, make it
asynchronous using Python 3,

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.073
using async IO And if
you are a Go programmer,

00:13:17.073 --> 00:13:18.490
you can basically
use Go routines.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:24.400
So this is asynchronous
representation

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:28.810
of the Cloud Function
that we have developed.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:31.660
And if I dive deep into
each of the functions

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:34.210
within the Cloud Function,
this is simple function

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:35.980
for object localization.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:38.590
All it does is it
takes the image input

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:40.250
from the GCS bucket.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:43.070
It instantiates an object
of Cloud Vision API.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:45.670
And all it receives
is coordinates

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:47.650
of the bounding
boxes, which are just

00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:49.600
the vertices of
the bounding boxes.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:54.520
That's what we get as a response
out of object localization.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:58.950
The next API is, we are actually
using object localization,

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:03.647
which is label detection, which
is basically assigning labels

00:14:03.647 --> 00:14:05.980
based on the objects that are
detected within the image.

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.410
The third one is web
entity detection.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:15.695
So for the image that you
actually give it as input,

00:14:15.695 --> 00:14:17.570
it actually searches on
the web and gives you

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:20.060
labels for the image.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:23.530
And finally, we have a
function for generating

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.280
signed URLs for the
images from Cloud Storage,

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:32.000
so that we can populate
that on top of the UI.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:36.610
So if you observe, in Node,js,
what we're using is we are

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.620
implementing promises.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:40.210
So basically, your
promise objects,

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:43.180
once they get resolved in
an asynchronous fashion,

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:45.610
we actually collate
all the responses,

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:48.490
create an image
document, which has

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:50.950
all the responses
from object detection,

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:53.550
to label detection, to
web entity direction,

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.270
to your signed URL.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:57.030
And we basically
write this object

00:14:57.030 --> 00:15:01.420
inside a predefined
collection in Cloud Firestore.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:04.640
All right, so let's
look at the demo.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:06.220
SHYAM GOVARDHAN: Great.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:09.310
Who is 100% believer
that live demos

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:12.700
at large conferences in front
of large audiences always work?

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:19.260
Well, we're going to
find out very soon.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:24.900
So what you're looking at here
is a smart environment monitor

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:27.880
and an object-detection system.

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:33.660
So essentially, it's a rig
that are put up together,

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:37.670
and it features a
Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+.

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:40.290
Who's played with one of these?

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:41.250
Many of you.

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:42.690
OK, good.

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:47.840
Who's played with a Sense HAT?

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:49.170
Good, a few of you.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:55.280
So you see the Raspberry Pi 3
Model B+ has those GPIO pins

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:56.930
at the top.

00:15:56.930 --> 00:16:01.900
The Sense HAT here comes
with a bunch of sensors.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.550
But the ones that I was
particularly interested in

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:07.800
is a gyroscope and
an accelerometer.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.880
So what you can do is
essentially take the Raspberry

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:14.300
Pi and the Sense HAT and
connect them both together

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:16.715
like so using the GPIO pins.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:22.280
And the third piece to
the puzzle is what's known

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:24.890
as a GrovePi+.

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:28.400
You can pick up the Raspberry
Pi 3 Model B+ for about 35 US

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.500
dollars.

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:34.130
Sense HAT is about
the same cost as well.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:39.620
And this thing here,
GrovePi+, is from Seed Studio,

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:44.540
and it comes with a few
connectors that allow you

00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:46.640
to hook up sensors.

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:48.890
So you have three
analog sensors,

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:50.780
connectors to the left.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:54.380
You have digital sensors,
connectors at the bottom.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:56.510
And you also have
other I2C connectors

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:58.410
on the right as well.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:03.350
So essentially, the Sense
HAT and the GrovePi+ are

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:07.010
essentially know as HATs,
Hardware Attached at the Top.

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:08.869
So you can literally
take it and hook it

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:13.290
up directly on top of one
another, just like so.

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:16.819
And what I've ended up with
is basically exactly what

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:19.910
I've described over there.

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:24.410
And what I also have
is a bunch of sensors

00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:27.500
connected to the entire rig.

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:32.520
So I have a light sensor on
the top left-hand corner.

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:34.490
It basically
detects fluctuations

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:36.280
in the light levels.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:40.760
The sounds sensor, obviously,
detects the fluctuations

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:42.470
in the sound level.

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:44.880
The angle sensor, it's a knob.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.720
It basically allows you
to twist it left or right.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.840
And I'm using it to control
the rate of transmitting

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:53.680
telemetry data to Firestore.

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:56.750
You know, essentially, it's a
potentiometer, in electronics

00:17:56.750 --> 00:17:57.750
terms.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:02.430
So when you turn it all the
way up, it sends it fast.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:06.320
And when you turn it
down, it sends it slower.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:10.610
And here, you basically
have a flame sensor.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.250
I was told that I couldn't
light a match or a lighter

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:14.960
here on stage, for
safety reasons,

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:17.390
so it ain't going to happen.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:20.330
You also have a
button here as well.

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:27.260
When you press the button, the
web camera will take a photo.

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:30.080
And there is an
ultrasonic sensor,

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:34.140
and that sensor basically
checks the proximity reading.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:38.480
So as you move an object
further or closer to it,

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.730
it will detect that.

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:45.500
And what actually happens
is, all of these readings

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:50.550
get uploaded, transmitted
to Firestore in real time.

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:52.160
And that's the beauty about it.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:55.040
What use is an
environment monitor

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:56.122
if it's not in real time?

00:18:56.122 --> 00:18:57.830
You don't want to know
that your house is

00:18:57.830 --> 00:18:59.450
on fire five minutes ago.

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:01.250
You want to know if
anything is happening

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:03.060
right this very moment.

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:05.600
So real time nature is critical.

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.570
And it's for that very
reason I felt that Firestore

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.112
was a perfect fit.

00:19:10.112 --> 00:19:12.320
I can't think of any other
solution that will give me

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:15.600
that feature and functionality.

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.150
So essentially,
this is basically

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:19.550
what my first
version looked like.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:21.320
I plugged in all the sensors.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:23.360
And as it says here,
my first version

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:28.220
also used Google IoT Core
and Google Pub/Sub as well.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:31.610
Pub/Sub is an asynchronous
message-broking platform

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:34.490
from Google, which is part
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:34.490 --> 00:19:36.530
And Google IoT
Core or allows you

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:39.350
to create a device
registry, a central registry

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:41.450
of all your IoT devices.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:44.110
For this particular
use case, I just

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:49.700
stuck with Firestore, because
it was much simpler to demo.

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:53.930
And this here was the final
version of what I ended up.

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:56.450
So the previous version of
